A Kickstart the CPU cannot run no longer kills the host process

memory_reset() checks the loaded Kickstart against the CPU (the A1200 and A4000 ROMs need a
68020, the A3000 and A4000T ones a 68030) and, like WinUAE, answers a mismatch with
notify_user() and uae_restart(). WinUAE goes back to its GUI. This port has none:

- notify_user() is an empty stub in retrodep/gui.c, so nothing is reported;
- uae_restart() only sets quit_program to -UAE_QUIT, so real_main2() returns before it sets
  UAE_RESET;
- retro_load_game() calls m68k_go() regardless, whose reset block runs only for a positive
  quit_program. build_cpufunctbl() and custom_reset() never run, and the first instruction is a
  call through an empty cpufunctbl_noret[] - an access violation at address 0 in the host.

Reproduced with Peekaboo on an A500 (68000) given kick40068.A1200: m68k_run_1_ce+646 calls
0x0, cpufunctbl[] and cpufunctbl_noret[] all NULL, and the core log has no "Building CPU" or
"Reset at" line.

Under __LIBRETRO__ the same four checks now log the mismatch at RETRO_LOG_ERROR (write_log is
INFO, which a front end may filter) and the load carries on: the machine boots the ROM and fails
as the real hardware would. Other builds keep WinUAE's behaviour.

Applies to libretro-uae 2b0ed42fe565fb997a0627aaa8f44e0948b527f8 in the order given by
patches/series.txt.

